<section xml:id="sec-fem-mc-intro" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction</title>

  <p> Radiative transfer models originate in the linear Boltzmann equation, a high-dimensional integro-differential equation whose solution describes the distribution of particles as they stream, scatter, and are absorbed through a medium.
  Obtaining a numerical solution is made challenging not only due to high dimensionality: collision operators take the form of <em>{nonlocal}</em> integrals, requiring knowledge of the solution across the entire spatial domain. 
  In applying finite element methods (FEM) directly, these nested integrals would yield a dense coupling between degrees of freedom, dominating the computational cost~\cite{craus2020accelerating}.
  </p>

</section>